e":"Variable-size sliding window over a string","lc":3},{"id":"longest-repeating-replacement","title":"Longest Repeating Character Replacement","subtitle":"Variable window · invariant len − maxFreq ≤ k","lc":424},{"id":"min-window-substring","title":"Minimum Window Substring","subtitle":"Smallest window of s covering all of t","lc":76},{"id":"permutation-in-string","title":"Permutation in String","subtitle":"Fixed-size window + frequency match","lc":567},{"id":"sliding-window-maximum","title":"Sliding Window Maximum","subtitle":"Monotonic deque · the front is always the window max","lc":239}]},{"id":"stack","title":"Stack","problems":[{"id":"intro","title":"Overview","subtitle":"LIFO · push, pop, and why \"the top\" matters"},{"id":"valid-parentheses","title":"Valid Parentheses","subtitle":"The top is the bracket that must close next","lc":20},{"id":"baseball-game","title":"Baseball Game","subtitle":"Every operation is about the top","lc":682},{"id":"decode-string","title":"Decode String","subtitle":"k[body] with nesting · \"]\" collapses the top","lc":394},{"id":"longest-valid-parentheses","title":"Longest Valid Parentheses","subtitle":"Stack of indices · a base below every run","lc":32},{"id":"monotonic-intro","title":"Monotonic Stack","subtitle":"Keep it sorted · bigger arrivals resolve the waiters"},{"id":"daily-temperatures","title":"Daily Temperatures","subtitle":"Monotonic stack · new days resolve waiting days","lc":739},{"id":"largest-rectangle-histogram","title":"Largest Rectangle in Histogram","subtitle":"Increasing stack · shorter bars close rectangles","lc":84},{"id":"min-stack","title":"Min Stack","subtitle":"O(1) getMin · a twin min-stack","lc":155},{"id":"evaluate-rpn","title":"Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation","subtitle":"Operand stack · postfix evaluation","lc":150}]},{"id":"linked-list","title":"Linked List","problems":[{"id":"intro","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Nodes, next-pointers, and O(1) surgery"},{"id":"reverse-linked-list","title":"Reverse Linked List","subtitle":"Flip every arrow · prev / curr / next","lc":206},{"id":"merge-two-sorted-lists","title":"Merge Two Sorted Lists","subtitle":"Splice the smaller head · dummy node","lc":21},{"id":"add-two-numbers","title":"Add Two Numbers","subtitle":"Elementary addition · carry","lc":2},{"id":"linked-list-cycle","title":"Linked List Cycle","subtitle":"Floyd's tortoise & hare","lc":141},{"id":"palindrome-linked-list","title":"Palindrome Linked List","subtitle":"Middle + reverse half + two pointers","lc":234},{"id":"remove-nth-from-end","title":"Remove Nth Node From End","subtitle":"Two pointers locked n apart","lc":19},{"id":"reorder-list","title":"Reorder List","subtitle":"Middle + reverse + alternate merge","lc":143},{"id":"swap-nodes-in-pairs","title":"Swap Nodes in Pairs","subtitle":"Three arrows per pair · dummy head","lc":24},{"id":"lru-cache","title":"LRU Cache","subtitle":"Hash map + doubly linked list · O(1) get / put","lc":146}]},{"id":"heap","title":"Heap","problems":[{"id":"intro","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Complete tree + heap property · sift-up / sift-down"},{"id":"kth-largest-element","title":"Kth Largest Element in an Array","subtitle":"Min-heap of size k as a top-k gate","lc":215},{"id":"k-closest-points","title":"K Closest Points to Origin","subtitle":"Max-heap of size k, keyed by distance","lc":973},{"id":"find-k-closest-elements","title":"Find K Closest Elements","subtitle":"Max-heap keyed by (distance, value)","lc":658},{"id":"merge-k-sorted-lists","title":"Merge K Sorted Lists","subtitle":"Min-heap of the k current heads","lc":23},{"id":"median-from-data-stream","title":"Median from Data Stream","subtitle":"Two heaps straddling the middle","lc":295},{"id":"task-scheduler","title":"Task Scheduler","subtitle":"Most-frequent-first · max-heap vs. the frame formula","lc":621},{"id":"relative-ranks","title":"Relative Ranks","subtitle":"Sort the indices so the scores keep their owners","lc":506}]},{"id":"binary-search","title":"Binary Search","problems":[{"id":"intro","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Halve the search space · then search answers, not arrays"},{"id":"binary-search","title":"Binary Search","subtitle":"The canonical template · halve [lo, hi]","lc":704},{"id":"search-insert-position","title":"Search Insert Position","subtitle":"Where lo lands when the target is missing","lc":35},{"id":"peak-index-mountain","title":"Peak Index in a Mountain Array","subtitle":"Binary search without a sorted array","lc":852},{"id":"max-candies-k-children","title":"Maximum Candies Allocated to K Children","subtitle":"Binary search the answer, not the array","lc":2226},{"id":"koko-eating-bananas","title":"Apple Harvest (Koko Eating Bananas)","subtitle":"Binary search on the answer · feasibility probe","lc":875},{"id":"search-rotated-sorted","title":"Search in Rotated Sorted Array","subtitle":"One half is always sorted, use it to steer","lc":33},{"id":"find-min-rotated","title":"Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array","subtitle":"Compare mid to hi to find the cliff","lc":153},{"id":"search-2d-matrix","title":"Search a 2D Matrix","subtitle":"Treat the grid as one flat sorted array · binary search 0..m·n−1","lc":74},{"id":"split-array-largest-sum","title":"Split Array Largest Sum","subtitle":"Binary search the cap · greedy feasibility","lc":410},{"id":"kth-smallest-sorted-matrix","title":"Kth Smallest in a Sorted Matrix","subtitle":"Binary search on values · count ≤ x probes","lc":378},{"id":"minimum-shipping-capacity","title":"Minimum Shipping Capacity","subtitle":"Ship packages in D days · search the capacity","lc":1011}]},{"id":"dfs","title":"Depth-First Search","problems":[{"id":"introduction","title":"Introduction","subtitle":"Go deep, then backtrack · pre/in/post order"},{"id":"fundamentals","title":"Fundamentals","subtitle":"The recursion template: base case → recurse → combine"},{"id":"return-values","title":"Return Values","subtitle":"Bubbling answers UP with post-order"},{"id":"maximum-depth","title":"Maximum Depth of Binary Tree","subtitle":"Depth = 1 + max(left, right)","lc":104},{"id":"path-sum","title":"Path Sum","subtitle":"Root-to-leaf sum == target · carry the remainder down","lc":112},{"id":"passing-values","title":"Passing Values Down and Up","subtitle":"Parameters go down, returns come up"},{"id":"validate-bst","title":"Validate Binary Search Tree","subtitle":"Carry (low, high) bounds down","lc":98},{"id":"calculate-tilt","title":"Binary Tree Tilt","subtitle":"Post-order: return subtree sums, accumulate |L − R|","lc":563},{"id":"diameter","title":"Diameter of a Binary Tree","subtitle":"Post-order heights, track the widest split","lc":543},{"id":"path-sum-ii","title":"Path Sum II","subtitle":"Backtracking: collect every root-to-leaf path == target","lc":113},{"id":"longest-univalue-path","title":"Longest Univalue Path","subtitle":"Longest chain of equal values","lc":687},{"id":"invert-tree","title":"Invert Binary Tree","subtitle":"Pre-order DFS, swap every node's children","lc":226},{"id":"same-tree","title":"Same Tree","subtitle":"Lockstep pre-order DFS over both trees","lc":100},{"id":"lowest-common-ancestor","title":"Lowest Common Ancestor of a Binary Tree","subtitle":"Post-order DFS, bubble a target up, split point wins","lc":236},{"id":"binary-tree-max-path-sum","title":"Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum","subtitle":"Post-order gains · clamp negative arms","lc":124},{"id":"serialize-deserialize","title":"Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree","subtitle":"Pre-order with null markers, then rebuild from the stream","lc":297},{"id":"graphs-overview","title":"Graphs Overview","subtitle":"Nodes, edges, and why DFS needs a visited set"},{"id":"adjacency-list","title":"Adjacency List","subtitle":"How to store a graph"},{"id":"copy-graph","title":"Copy Graph","subtitle":"Deep clone with an old→new map","lc":133},{"id":"graph-valid-tree","title":"Graph Valid Tree","subtitle":"Connected + exactly n−1 edges + no cycle","lc":261},{"id":"grid-dfs-overview","title":"Matrices","subtitle":"A grid is a graph · DFS the 4 neighbours"},{"id":"flood-fill","title":"Flood Fill","subtitle":"Recolour a connected region","lc":733},{"id":"number-of-islands","title":"Number of Islands","subtitle":"Count components, sink each island","lc":200},{"id":"surrounded-regions","title":"Surrounded Regions","subtitle":"Border-connected O's are safe","lc":130},{"id":"pacific-atlantic","title":"Pacific Atlantic Water Flow","subtitle":"Flood inward from both oceans, intersect","lc":417}]},{"id":"greedy","title":"Greedy Algorithms","problems":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Take the best local choice, never look back"},{"id":"best-time-buy-sell-stock","title":"Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock","subtitle":"Track the cheapest day seen so far","lc":121},{"id":"gas-station","title":"Gas Station","subtitle":"If total gas ≥ total cost, one start works, find it","lc":134},{"id":"jump-game","title":"Jump Game","subtitle":"Track the farthest index you can reach","lc":55},{"id":"jump-game-ii","title":"Jump Game II","subtitle":"Minimum jumps, greedy BFS by levels","lc":45},{"id":"partition-labels","title":"Partition Labels","subtitle":"Extend the part to each letter’s last occurrence","lc":763}]},{"id":"dp","title":"Dynamic Programming","problems":[{"id":"fundamentals","title":"Fundamentals","subtitle":"Overlapping subproblems · a table you fill once"},{"id":"solving-with-dp","title":"Solving a Question with Dynamic Programming","subtitle":"State → recurrence → base case → order → answer"},{"id":"pascals-triangle","title":"Pascal's Triangle","subtitle":"The smallest recurrence there is","lc":118},{"id":"climbing-stairs","title":"Climbing Stairs","subtitle":"ways(i) = ways(i−1) + ways(i−2) · Fibonacci","lc":70},{"id":"dice-combinations","title":"Dice Combinations","subtitle":"dp[i] = Σ dp[i−j] for j in 1..6 · a 6-wide window","lc":377},{"id":"max-subarray","title":"Maximum Subarray","subtitle":"cur = max(nums[i], cur + nums[i]) · Kadane's","lc":53},{"id":"house-robber","title":"House Robber","subtitle":"dp[i] = max(dp[i−1], dp[i−2] + nums[i])","lc":198},{"id":"coin-change","title":"Coin Change","subtitle":"dp[a] = 1 + min(dp[a − coin]) · fewest coins to make amount","lc":322},{"id":"longest-common-subsequence","title":"Longest Common Subsequence","subtitle":"Grid DP · match → diagonal+1, else max(up, left)","lc":1143},{"id":"edit-distance","title":"Edit Distance","subtitle":"Grid DP · dp[i][j] = min edits word1[:i] → word2[:j]","lc":72},{"id":"counting-bits","title":"Counting Bits","subtitle":"dp[i] = dp[i>>1] + (i & 1)","lc":338},{"id":"decode-ways","title":"Decode Ways","subtitle":"1 or 2 digits at a time","lc":91},{"id":"unique-paths","title":"Unique Paths","subtitle":"Grid DP · dp[i][j] = dp[i−1][j] + dp[i][j−1]","lc":62},{"id":"maximal-square","title":"Maximal Square","subtitle":"Grid DP · dp[i][j] = min(up, left, diag) + 1","lc":221},{"id":"longest-increasing-subsequence","title":"Longest Increasing Subsequence","subtitle":"dp[i] = longest increasing run ENDING at i","lc":300},{"id":"word-break","title":"Word Break","subtitle":"dp[i] = can s[0..i) be split into dictionary words","lc":139},{"id":"maximum-profit-job-scheduling","title":"Maximum Profit in Job Scheduling","subtitle":"Sort by end; take or skip","lc":1235},{"id":"paint-house","title":"Paint House","subtitle":"Grid DP · dp[i][c] = cost + min(other two colours)","lc":256},{"id":"paint-house-ii","title":"Paint House II","subtitle":"k colours · track the two smallest → O(n·k)","lc":265},{"id":"minimum-window-subsequence","title":"Minimum Window Subsequence","subtitle":"Smallest window of S containing T as a subsequence","lc":727}]},{"id":"graphs","title":"Graphs","problems":[{"id":"graphs-overview","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Directed, weighted, and how we store them"},{"id":"course-schedule","title":"Course Schedule","subtitle":"Can you finish? = is it a DAG (no cycle)?","lc":207},{"id":"course-schedule-ii","title":"Course Schedule II","subtitle":"Return a valid topological order","lc":210},{"id":"shortest-path-algorithms","title":"Shortest Path Algorithms","subtitle":"BFS vs Dijkstra vs Bellman-Ford"},{"id":"network-delay-time","title":"Network Delay Time","subtitle":"Dijkstra · finalize the nearest unfinished node","lc":743},{"id":"cheapest-flights","title":"Cheapest Flights Within K Stops","subtitle":"Bellman-Ford bounded to K+1 hops","lc":787},{"id":"path-minimum-effort","title":"Path With Minimum Effort","subtitle":"Dijkstra on a grid · minimize the max step","lc":1631},{"id":"find-the-city","title":"Find the City With Fewest Reachable","subtitle":"Floyd-Warshall all-pairs, then count","lc":1334},{"id":"union-find","title":"Union-Find (DSU)","subtitle":"Disjoint sets · find + union"},{"id":"connected-components","title":"Number of Connected Components","subtitle":"Union-Find · count = n, merge drops count","lc":323},{"id":"redundant-connection","title":"Redundant Connection","subtitle":"Union-Find · first cycle-closing edge","lc":684},{"id":"word-ladder","title":"Word Ladder","subtitle":"Shortest transformation sequence via BFS","lc":127}]},{"id":"backtracking","title":"Backtracking","problems":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Choose → explore → un-choose"},{"id":"word-search","title":"Word Search","subtitle":"DFS with mark-and-unmark backtracking","lc":79},{"id":"solution-space-trees","title":"Solution Space Trees","subtitle":"Nodes = partial solutions, edges = choices"},{"id":"subsets","title":"Subsets","subtitle":"Every node of the tree is a subset","lc":78},{"id":"permutations","title":"Permutations","subtitle":"Choose an unused element at each level","lc":46},{"id":"letter-combinations","title":"Letter Combinations of a Phone Number","subtitle":"One digit per tree level","lc":17},{"id":"generate-parentheses","title":"Generate Parentheses","subtitle":"Add '(' or ')' with validity pruning","lc":22},{"id":"combination-sum","title":"Combination Sum","subtitle":"Reuse allowed; prune when the sum overshoots","lc":39},{"id":"palindrome-partitioning","title":"Palindrome Partitioning","subtitle":"Cut only where the prefix is a palindrome","lc":131},{"id":"n-queens","title":"N-Queens","subtitle":"Place row by row, backtrack on attack","lc":51}]},{"id":"bfs","title":"Breadth-First Search","problems":[{"id":"introduction","title":"Introduction","subtitle":"Level by level with a FIFO queue"},{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Process one whole level per iteration"},{"id":"level-order-sum","title":"Level Order Sum","subtitle":"Sum each level with the level-batching loop","lc":1161},{"id":"rightmost-node","title":"Rightmost Node","subtitle":"Right-side view = last node of each level","lc":199},{"id":"zigzag-level-order","title":"Zigzag Level Order","subtitle":"Alternate L→R, R→L each level","lc":103},{"id":"maximum-width","title":"Maximum Width of Binary Tree","subtitle":"Index nodes by position 2i, 2i+1","lc":662},{"id":"graphs-overview","title":"Graphs Overview","subtitle":"BFS finds the shortest path (fewest edges)"},{"id":"minimum-knight-moves","title":"Minimum Knight Moves","subtitle":"BFS = shortest path on the move-graph","lc":1197},{"id":"rotting-oranges","title":"Rotting Oranges","subtitle":"Multi-source BFS · rot spreads one ring per minute","lc":994},{"id":"01-matrix","title":"01 Matrix","subtitle":"Distance to nearest 0 · multi-source BFS","lc":542},{"id":"bus-routes","title":"Bus Routes","subtitle":"BFS over routes: each level = one more bus","lc":815}]},{"id":"trie","title":"Trie","problems":[{"id":"intro","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Prefix tree · shared prefixes stored once"},{"id":"implement-trie","title":"Implement Trie Methods","subtitle":"insert · search · startsWith","lc":208},{"id":"prefix-matching","title":"Prefix Matching","subtitle":"Autocomplete · walk prefix, collect subtree","lc":1268}]},{"id":"prefix-sum","title":"Prefix Sum","problems":[{"id":"intro","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Precompute running totals · range sum in O(1)"},{"id":"count-vowels-substrings","title":"Count Vowels in Substrings","subtitle":"Prefix-sum a 0/1 indicator"},{"id":"subarray-sum-equals-k","title":"Subarray Sum Equals K","subtitle":"Running prefix + a count hashmap","lc":560}]},{"id":"matrices","title":"Matrices","problems":[{"id":"spiral-matrix","title":"Spiral Matrix","subtitle":"Four shrinking boundaries","lc":54},{"id":"rotate-image","title":"Rotate Image","subtitle":"90° in place · transpose+reverse or 4-cycles","lc":48},{"id":"set-matrix-zeroes","title":"Set Matrix Zeroes","subtitle":"Find then zero · markers in the first row/col","lc":73},{"id":"island-perimeter","title":"Island Perimeter","subtitle":"Count contributions, not the outline","lc":463},{"id":"missing-and-repeated","title":"Find Missing and Repeated Values","subtitle":"Two equations, two unknowns","lc":2965}]},{"id":"intervals","title":"Intervals","problems":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Sort, then sweep · overlap ⇔ b.start ≤ a.end"},{"id":"merge-intervals","title":"Merge Intervals","subtitle":"Sort by start · extend while overlapping","lc":56},{"id":"insert-interval","title":"Insert Interval","subtitle":"Sorted list · three-phase sweep · no sort","lc":57},{"id":"non-overlapping-intervals","title":"Non-overlapping Intervals","subtitle":"Sort by end · greedily keep early finishers","lc":435},{"id":"meeting-rooms","title":"Meeting Rooms","subtitle":"Sort by start · check neighbours overlap","lc":252},{"id":"meeting-rooms-ii","title":"Meeting Rooms II","subtitle":"Chronological sweep · peak overlap = rooms","lc":253}]},{"id":"bit-manipulation","title":"Bit Manipulation","problems":[{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","subtitle":"AND · OR · XOR · shifts · the classic tricks"},{"id":"single-number","title":"Single Number","subtitle":"XOR fold · pairs cancel","lc":136},{"id":"number-of-1-bits","title":"Number of 1 Bits","subtitle":"Hamming weight · n & (n − 1)","lc":191},{"id":"missing-number","title":"Missing Number","subtitle":"XOR index ⊕ value · pairs cancel","lc":268},{"id":"reverse-bits","title":"Reverse Bits","subtitle":"build the answer bit by bit","lc":190},{"id":"sum-of-two-integers","title":"Sum of Two Integers","subtitle":"XOR sum + carry · add without +","lc":371}]}],"activePattern":"graphs","activeProblem":"graphs-overview"}],"$L19"]}]
1a:I[28440,["2619","static/chunks/2619-04bc32f026a0d946.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","6489","static/chunks/6489-699922ea493f9867.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","1356","static/chunks/1356-9e2757ba007ad1f9.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","951","static/chunks/951-3dbda3a86d4c6444.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","2799","static/chunks/2799-ce430b82b11995d5.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","6589","static/chunks/6589-cfdc115b042e146b.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","3650","static/chunks/3650-106e80ed926f59bf.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","8440","static/chunks/8440-bfb72547be677d52.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","134","static/chunks/app/%5Bpattern%5D/%5BproblemId%5D/page-f9e02a54f5641ad9.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs"],"AnimationRunner"]
19:["$","$L1a","graphs-overview",{"problem":{"id":"graphs-overview","patternId":"graphs","title":"Overview","subtitle":"Directed, weighted, and how we store them","kind":"concept","input":[],"render":"graph","approaches":[{"label":"Graph vocabulary + a BFS recap","complexity":{"time":"O(V + E)","space":"O(V + E)"},"pseudocode":["// a graph = nodes + edges","edges may be directed and/or weighted","in-degree(v)  = edges arriving at v","out-degree(v) = edges leaving v","a DAG = directed graph with no cycle","store as adjacency list: adj[u] = [neighbours]","// traverse (BFS):","seen = {source}; queue = [source]","while queue: u = dequeue","    for v in adj[u]: if v unseen: enqueue v","done, every reachable node visited"],"steps":[{"codeLine":1,"narration":"A graph is a set of NODES (also called vertices) joined by EDGES. Unlike a tree there is no root and edges may form cycles. Almost every graph problem is one of three flavours: REACHABILITY (can I get from A to B?), ORDERING (what sequence respects the dependencies?), or SHORTEST PATH (what is the cheapest route?). Learn the vocabulary first.","graph":{"nodes":[{"id":"0","label":0,"x":0,"y":0},{"id":"1","label":1,"x":2,"y":0},{"id":"2","label":2,"x":0,"y":1.6},{"id":"3","label":3,"x":2,"y":1.6}],"edges":[{"from":"0","to":"1","directed":true,"weight":5},{"from":"0","to":"2","directed":true,"weight":2},{"from":"2","to":"1","directed":true,"weight":1},{"from":"2","to":"3","directed":true,"weight":7},{"from":"1","to":"3","directed":true,"weight":3}],"visitedIds":[],"frontierIds":[],"adjacency":[{"node":"0","neighbors":["1","2"]},{"node":"1","neighbors":["3"]},{"node":"2","neighbors":["1","3"]},{"node":"3","neighbors":[]}],"label":"directed, weighted graph"},"vars":[["nodes (V)",4],["edges (E)",5]]},{"codeLine":2,"narration":"These edges are DIRECTED: each arrowhead means the edge goes one way only. 0→1 lets you walk from 0 to 1, but NOT from 1 to 0. An UNDIRECTED edge would be a two-way street drawn without an arrow. Direction is what turns \"friends\" (mutual) into \"follows\" (one-way).","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","visitedIds":[],"frontierIds":[],"adjacency":[{"node":"0","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:0:neighbors"},{"node":"1","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:1:neighbors"},{"node":"2","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:2:neighbors"},{"node":"3","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:3:neighbors"}],"label":"directed, weighted graph","activeId":"0","highlightIds":["1"],"edgeHighlights":[["0","1"]]},"vars":[["edge 0→1","one-way"],["directed?","yes"]]},{"codeLine":3,"narration":"Each edge also carries a WEIGHT, the number in the badge. A weight is the cost of crossing that edge: distance, time, price, anything. 0→1 costs 5 but the detour 0→2→1 costs 2+1 = 3, which is cheaper. Weights are exactly why shortest-path is interesting; without them \"shortest\" just means \"fewest edges\".","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","visitedIds":[],"frontierIds":[],"adjacency":[{"node":"0","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:0:neighbors"},{"node":"1","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:1:neighbors"},{"node":"2","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:2:neighbors"},{"node":"3","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:3:neighbors"}],"label":"directed, weighted graph","edgeHighlights":[["0","2"],["2","1"]],"highlightIds":["2"]},"vars":[["0→1 direct",5],["0→2→1",3]]},{"codeLine":4,"narration":"Each node has a DEGREE. Out-degree = how many edges LEAVE it; in-degree = how many edges ARRIVE. Node 0 has out-degree 2 (→1, →2) and in-degree 0 (nothing points at it). A node with in-degree 0 is a natural starting point, nothing must come before it. We will lean on exactly this idea for topological sort.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","visitedIds":[],"frontierIds":[],"adjacency":[{"node":"0","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:0:neighbors"},{"node":"1","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:1:neighbors"},{"node":"2","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:2:neighbors"},{"node":"3","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:3:neighbors"}],"label":"directed, weighted graph","activeId":"0","edgeHighlights":[["0","1"],["0","2"]],"notes":{"0":"out=2 in=0"}},"vars":[["out-degree(0)",2],["in-degree(0)",0]]},{"codeLine":5,"narration":"Node 3 is the mirror image: in-degree 2 (1→3 and 2→3 both arrive) and out-degree 0 (no edges leave). A node with out-degree 0 is a SINK, a dead end. 0 is a source, 3 is a sink. Real graphs often have several of each.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","visitedIds":[],"frontierIds":[],"adjacency":[{"node":"0","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:0:neighbors"},{"node":"1","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:1:neighbors"},{"node":"2","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:2:neighbors"},{"node":"3","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:3:neighbors"}],"label":"directed, weighted graph","activeId":"3","edgeHighlights":[["1","3"],["2","3"]],"notes":{"3":"in=2 out=0"}},"vars":[["in-degree(3)",2],["out-degree(3)",0]]},{"codeLine":6,"narration":"A PATH is a sequence of nodes connected by edges, each following the arrows: 0→2→1→3 is a valid path. A CYCLE is a path that returns to where it started. This graph has NO cycle, you can never get back to a node you left. A directed graph with no cycle is called a DAG, and DAGs are the graphs you can put in a linear order.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","visitedIds":[],"frontierIds":[],"adjacency":[{"node":"0","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:0:neighbors"},{"node":"1","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:1:neighbors"},{"node":"2","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:2:neighbors"},{"node":"3","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:3:neighbors"}],"label":"directed, weighted graph","edgeHighlights":[["0","2"],["2","1"],["1","3"]],"highlightIds":["0","1","2","3"]},"vars":[["path","0→2→1→3"],["cycle?","none (DAG)"]]},{"codeLine":7,"narration":"CONNECTED COMPONENTS: a component is a clump of nodes you can reach from one another. Here every node is reachable from 0, so the graph is one component. If some nodes sat off on their own island, that island would be a second component, and a single traversal from 0 would miss it.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","visitedIds":[],"frontierIds":[],"adjacency":[{"node":"0","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:0:neighbors"},{"node":"1","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:1:neighbors"},{"node":"2","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:2:neighbors"},{"node":"3","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:3:neighbors"}],"label":"directed, weighted graph","highlightIds":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:5:graph:highlightIds"},"vars":[["components",1]]},{"codeLine":8,"narration":"How do we store a graph in code? The ADJACENCY LIST on the right: for each node, the list of nodes it points to. adj[0] = [1, 2], adj[3] = []. It uses O(V + E) space and lets us loop over a node's neighbours instantly, perfect for sparse graphs. (The alternative, an adjacency MATRIX, costs O(V²) and is only worth it when the graph is dense.)","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","visitedIds":[],"frontierIds":[],"adjacency":[{"node":"0","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:0:neighbors"},{"node":"1","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:1:neighbors"},{"node":"2","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:2:neighbors"},{"node":"3","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:3:neighbors"}],"label":"directed, weighted graph"},"vars":[["storage","adjacency list"],["space","O(V + E)"]]},{"codeLine":9,"narration":"Let us recap traversal with a BFS from 0. BFS uses a QUEUE (first-in, first-out) and explores in rings: all nodes 1 edge away, then 2 edges away, and so on. Start by enqueueing the source 0 and marking it seen. The queue holds the FRONTIER, nodes discovered but not yet expanded.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","visitedIds":[],"frontierIds":["0"],"adjacency":[{"node":"0","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:0:neighbors"},{"node":"1","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:1:neighbors"},{"node":"2","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:2:neighbors"},{"node":"3","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:3:neighbors"}],"label":"directed, weighted graph","activeId":"0","stackLabel":"queue (FIFO)","stack":[{"label":"0"}]},"vars":[["enqueue",0],["queue","[0]"]]},{"codeLine":10,"narration":"Dequeue 0 and mark it done (green). Scan its neighbours: 1, 2 are new, so enqueue them. We enqueue a node the moment we FIRST see it, which guarantees each node is queued once, that is what keeps BFS O(V + E).","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","visitedIds":["0"],"frontierIds":["1","2"],"adjacency":[{"node":"0","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:0:neighbors"},{"node":"1","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:1:neighbors"},{"node":"2","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:2:neighbors"},{"node":"3","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:3:neighbors"}],"label":"directed, weighted graph","activeId":"0","highlightIds":["1","2"],"edgeHighlights":[["0","1"],["0","2"]],"stackLabel":"queue (FIFO)","stack":[{"label":"1"},{"label":"2"}]},"vars":[["dequeue","0"],["new","1,2"],["queue","[1,2]"]]},{"codeLine":10,"narration":"Dequeue 1 and mark it done (green). Scan its neighbours: 3 is new, so enqueue it. We enqueue a node the moment we FIRST see it, which guarantees each node is queued once, that is what keeps BFS O(V + E).","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","visitedIds":["0","1"],"frontierIds":["2","3"],"adjacency":[{"node":"0","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:0:neighbors"},{"node":"1","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:1:neighbors"},{"node":"2","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:2:neighbors"},{"node":"3","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:3:neighbors"}],"label":"directed, weighted graph","activeId":"1","highlightIds":["3"],"edgeHighlights":[["1","3"]],"stackLabel":"queue (FIFO)","stack":[{"label":"2"},{"label":"3"}]},"vars":[["dequeue","1"],["new","3"],["queue","[2,3]"]]},{"codeLine":10,"narration":"Dequeue 2 and mark it done. All of 2's neighbours are already seen, so there is nothing new to enqueue.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","visitedIds":["0","1","2"],"frontierIds":["3"],"adjacency":[{"node":"0","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:0:neighbors"},{"node":"1","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:1:neighbors"},{"node":"2","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:2:neighbors"},{"node":"3","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:3:neighbors"}],"label":"directed, weighted graph","activeId":"2","highlightIds":[],"edgeHighlights":[],"stackLabel":"queue (FIFO)","stack":[{"label":"3"}]},"vars":[["dequeue","2"],["new",""],["queue","[3]"]]},{"codeLine":10,"narration":"Dequeue 3 and mark it done. All of 3's neighbours are already seen, so there is nothing new to enqueue.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","visitedIds":["0","1","2","3"],"frontierIds":[],"adjacency":[{"node":"0","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:0:neighbors"},{"node":"1","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:1:neighbors"},{"node":"2","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:2:neighbors"},{"node":"3","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:3:neighbors"}],"label":"directed, weighted graph","activeId":"3","highlightIds":[],"edgeHighlights":[],"stackLabel":"queue (FIFO)","stack":[]},"vars":[["dequeue","3"],["new",""],["queue","[]"]]},{"codeLine":11,"narration":"Every node is green and the queue is empty: BFS visited all 4 nodes in ring order from the source. Swap the queue for a STACK (or recursion) and the very same loop becomes DFS, which dives deep before backtracking. Both run in O(V + E). With this vocabulary in hand, directed, weighted, in/out-degree, paths, cycles, components, the named algorithms (topological sort, Dijkstra, Bellman-Ford) are just disciplined traversals.","graph":{"nodes":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:nodes","edges":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:edges","visitedIds":["0","1","2","3"],"frontierIds":[],"adjacency":[{"node":"0","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:0:neighbors"},{"node":"1","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:1:neighbors"},{"node":"2","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:2:neighbors"},{"node":"3","neighbors":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:0:graph:adjacency:3:neighbors"}],"label":"directed, weighted graph","highlightIds":"$19:props:problem:approaches:0:steps:5:graph:highlightIds","stackLabel":"queue (FIFO)","stack":[]},"vars":[["visited",4],["time","O(V + E)"]]}]}]},"patternTitle":"Graphs"}]
